{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/974073-help-me-understand-territory","id":974073,"title":"Help me understand Territory","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-09-21T15:40:28-07:00","id":974073,"title":"Help me understand Territory","content":"Let's say I've got a mid-sized city in midwest america -- let's call it &quot;Baseline City&quot; -- with a typical-to-high density of uratha, and I want to determine how pack territory looks on a map. Would I see filled map regions, with most all of the city covered by one pack or another, and sharing borders with other packs? Or would it look more like pushpin nodes, with very poorly defined edges? Or perhaps a third option, with well defined borders but also typically plenty of unclaimed buffer between themselves and the next closest packs?<br /> <br /> I'm also curious if werewolf territory overlap with the territory marked by other creatures? For the sake of simplicity, let' only assume the core 3 lines, plus maybe hunters? For example, let's say a prime strip of downtown clubs and bars is the local Rack for Kindred. Would you consider it more typical that werewolves would respect/accept that as &quot;vampire&quot; territory, or that the Rack would exist in some pack's territory, and that pack may or may not have a problem with it? Basically, i'm trying to get a sense of if werewolves recognize territory of other entities, or just other werewolves.<br /> <br /> Of course I know the answer to all of this is always &quot;it depends, whatever works for your game, etc&quot;, but I'd like to know what the average / median / bog-standard way of setting this up is so I've got a baseline to work with.<br /> <br /> I appreciate your opinions on the matter!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/358-emanantvolition","name":"EmanantVolition","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar358_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-21T16:09:39-07:00","id":974086,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>EmanantVolition</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/974073-help-me-understand-territory#post974073\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Let's say I've got a mid-sized city in midwest america -- let's call it &quot;Baseline City&quot; -- with a typical-to-high density of uratha, and I want to determine how pack territory looks on a map. Would I see filled map regions, with most all of the city covered by one pack or another, and sharing borders with other packs? Or would it look more like pushpin nodes, with very poorly defined edges? Or perhaps a third option, with well defined borders but also typically plenty of unclaimed buffer between themselves and the next closest packs?</div> </div> </div> </div>You got this in your third paragraph, it depends. There is no standard, we have three books that detail werewolf locations and none of them contain a map about this kind of thing. Each method has its pros and cons, and each can be justified. The first option, with the packs sharing borders, implies that it's packed to the brim and packs are very definitive about their borders. This may be justified, maybe they're well defended, or not, and maybe they just mark the edges and keep other werewolves out but don't fully control their land. A big con with this is that new packs have no space to start without being gifted a territory. I could see that option with protectorates controlling a specific area. The second option, I admit, is somewhat unlikely. There's a rite that defines the borders of ones' territory, and while not every werewolf has it, it's likely at least one pack in a high-density Uratha city would. The third, to me, seems the most likely. Some territories ritually bordered, some not, and a lot of unexplored space.<br /> <br /> If you want to look at the books depicting werewolf territories, keeping in mind they are first edition, there's <a href=\"http://whitewolf.wikia.com/wiki/World_of_Darkness:_Chicago\" target=\"_blank\">Chicago</a>, <a href=\"http://whitewolf.wikia.com/wiki/Hunting_Ground:_The_Rockies\" target=\"_blank\">Hunting Ground: The Rockies</a>, <a href=\"http://whitewolf.wikia.com/wiki/Shadows_of_the_UK\" target=\"_blank\">Shadows of the UK</a>, and the <a href=\"http://whitewolf.wikia.com/wiki/Territories\" target=\"_blank\">Territories book</a>.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm also curious if werewolf territory overlap with the territory marked by other creatures? For the sake of simplicity, let' only assume the core 3 lines, plus maybe hunters? For example, let's say a prime strip of downtown clubs and bars is the local Rack for Kindred. Would you consider it more typical that werewolves would respect/accept that as &quot;vampire&quot; territory, or that the Rack would exist in some pack's territory, and that pack may or may not have a problem with it? Basically, i'm trying to get a sense of if werewolves recognize territory of other entities, or just other werewolves. </div> </div> </div>Oh, totally overlaps. They do different things with different interests, and werewolves and vampires and mages don't have reason to hate each other unless one side is overly aggressive. Whether the actions of the other supernatural are accepted are going to depend on the werewolves involved. Obviously it's the type of situation that's ripe for a dramatic conflict.<br /> <br /> All that said, vampires and mages don't really claim territory the same way werewolves do. Werewolves tend to pick large areas, while vampires and mages pick specific locations, like the local Rack or Athenaeum.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 09-21-2016, 07:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-21T19:27:46-07:00","id":974189,"title":"","content":"Something to add:<br /> <br /> Not every location is going to consider territory strictly physical boundaries. Packs might claim specific industries, or types of people, etc. as territory more than physical space. One of the example Packs in The Pack, operates out of a firehouse, and their neighboring packs basically get out of the way when they come in their duty as first responders.<br /> <br /> So this can make things even more amorphous.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-21T19:55:05-07:00","id":974196,"title":"","content":"Absolutely.<br /> <br /> There's all sorts of oddities that make 'it depends' a much bigger statement than it seems. I know it can seem dismissive but there's just a lot out there. There's vampires who only feed from certain people like a family or the sick, there's werewolves who are entirely nomadic, mages who barge in and upend anyone else's concept of territory. There's members of each supernatural who will hate the others and want them dead. There's werewolves who don't respect the territories of others, and protectorates that may arrange for some sort of no-borders rule. Packs die, idigam pop up and turn wolves into heralds, vampires get possessed by strix or other things and their feeding requirements change, mages summon some insane hivemind that seeks to absorb all supernatural flesh into a blanket across the Earth. And as it is, territory isn't a wall, outside of certain magics nothing keeps someone from walking over a border except an appreciation for whoever controls the territory. No one's omniscient, it's possible to walk through a dozen territories and not realize it, or not be noticed doing so. There's also powers that help with this, however.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 09-21-2016, 10:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-29T01:54:55-07:00","id":978271,"title":"","content":"Something that comes into play is territorial markings. Graffiti, claw marks, runes and the like can display a territorial boundary. <br /> <br /> Personally I like buffer zones, but a full city would have territory packed out. Block by block claimed. Dont forget territorial battles amongst differing uratha packs. The forsaken will fight each other almost as readily as they'll fight for pure (wasn't that the brethren war?).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> WoD-Dark Eras!! (Backed for Viking Age Werewolf)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1939-lyrics-of-war","name":"Lyrics Of War","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1939_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1769"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-29T08:14:22-07:00","id":978418,"title":"","content":"Mentions of the more recent Brethren War usually mention that it came at a time when the Forsaken were 'at peace,' as they thought the Pure numbers were severely diminished after the last Brethren War. Forsaken proliferated and ended up falling to infighting a lot, due to their lack of an overt threat like the Pure, yes. The Pure took advantage of this lack of attention on them to recoup their numbers in the hidden places in the world, and spy on them, possibly instigating further infighting before actually stepping in on the weakened Forsaken and wiping much of them out.<br /> <br /> In several places it's considered a cyclical thing. At one point the Forsaken grow fat, happy and fall to infighting, then to an external opponent, then the Pure do.<br /> <br /> Second edition seems to be going away from certain 'canon' in history, and it seems like the concept of a Protectorate would be an attempt to forestall this kind of thing. Unfortunately protectorates seem to have the same problem, growing unwieldy then falling apart after time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}